  "body": "I knew css had ime-mode property. I understand why it's obsolete - it makes no sense that styling controlls keyboard's behavior.\r\nBut if html input element itself had it, it helps a lot. Think why ime-mode property was born. Some ways to control the input ime is sought-after. You can see how many Japanese web developpers wanted the feature via [Google](https://www.google.com/search?q=input+text+%E5%85%A8%E8%A7%92+%E7%A6%81%E6%AD%A2&rlz=1C1GCEV_enJP860JP860&ei=IPkAYbrfD9KLr7wPqfeqsAI&oq=input+text+%E3%81%9C%E3%82%93%E3%81%8B%E3%81%8F&gs_lcp=Cgdnd3Mtd2l6EAEYADIECAAQBDIGCAAQBBAeMgYIABAEEB4yBggAEAQQHjIICAAQCBAEEB4yCAgAEAgQBBAeOggIABCwAxDNAjoGCAAQBBADOgoIABCxAxCDARAEOgUIABCxAzoHCAAQsQMQBDoKCAAQsQMQgwEQQzoECAAQQzoICAAQsQMQgwE6AggAOggIABAFEAQQHkoECEEYAVDnlRZYuLAWYOzAFmgCcAB4AIABlwKIAaUfkgEGMC44LjExmAEAoAEBqgEHZ3dzLXdpesgBAcABAQ&sclient=gws-wiz).\r\nThe name might not be appropriate. I think \"canCompose\" is better because it responds to input's compositionstart, compositionupdate, and compositionend events.",
